<p>CHAPTER 3. THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK: EPISTEMIC SYSTEMS THEORY 56
3.9
Summary of the Framework
EST integrates the reviewed concerns into four linked propositions: architectural
inuence and its proposed dominance (EST-P1), conditional network-diversity
constraints (EST-P2), measurement accountability (EST-P3), and aordance-
dependent participation and evidence-based production (EST-P4). The proposed
dominance of architecture and the benets of individual aordances remain open to
direct tests. The framework contains both empirical commitments and a normative
design requirement; a single positive correlation cannot validate them together.
Chapter 4 species the proposed platform mechanisms, and Chapter 5 distinguishes
the evidence available for each assessment.
3.9.1
The Formal Operator Bank
The nal computational specication assembles the theoretical mechanisms into
eight composable operators. They are introduced here so that the mathematics of
Chapter 3 is visible before the implementation in Chapter 5. The operators are
formal modelling devices: the Hamiltonian, entropy, and Lyapunov terms are analo-
gies and diagnostics, not claims that social media obey physical thermodynamics.
Table 5.8 in Section 5.5.6 gives the governing expressions and their EST mapping.
The bank has three functions. First, it makes the architecture executable:
each subsystem corresponds to a named operator rather than a verbal slogan. Sec-
ond, it separates the mechanisms so that the 23 design can switch them on and o
independently. Third, it creates falsiable diagnostics: if an operator has no eect
on the measured endpoints under the calibrated model, that is evidence against the</p>
<p>CHAPTER 3. THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK: EPISTEMIC SYSTEMS THEORY 57
mechanism as specied. The operator bank is therefore a bridge between EST's
propositions and the computational test, not a substitute for empirical validation.
Chapter summary. EST is presented as a design theory, not a proven law.
The chapter turns its propositions into formal objects: a reputation update,
a diversity/echo constraint, a measurement model, and participation rules.
The Hamiltonian and Zewail analogies are specication devices. The chapter
supplies the hypotheses and the language used in the simulation, and it states
what would count as evidence against each proposition.</p>
