5.6.1
Field-Wave Procedure
1. Eligibility and recruitment.
Participants were university students who
used Uber rides in the October 6th City area, Giza. Participation was volun-
tary and anonymous; names and phone numbers were not retained with the
research data. Each record carries an anonymous research ID.
2. Consent. The consent screen preceded the questionnaire. It stated the pur-
pose, the voluntary nature, the right to withdraw, and the separation of LOK-
LOK prole data from research responses.
3. Ride context. Respondents recorded ride duration, interaction level during
the ride, and the listening context. The observed range was 515 minutes;
interaction was reported as minimal, low, moderate, or high.
4. Listening episode. During the ride, respondents used the LOKLOK eld
interface to listen to a social-media radio episode.
The interface displayed
the show, broadcaster, BRS badge, credibility interval, and Echo Meter where
available.
The eld wave is a post-broadcast cross-sectional survey, not a
session-randomised trial.
5. Post-broadcast questionnaire. After the episode, respondents completed
the star rating, the 11-item BRS battery (BR1BR4, SV1SV4, IQ1IQ3; 0
4 agreement, BR4 reverse-coded), and the ve-item PB battery (PB1PB5;
17). The star rating and PB were separate blocks.
6. Export and screening. The raw export contained 60 records. The historical
21-eld key was applied to identify duplicate response vectors; eight records
were removed, leaving 52 for the primary analysis. The key identies duplicate</p>
<p>CHAPTER 5. METHODOLOGY
112
vectors, not independently veried duplicate people. The 60-row le is retained
as a sensitivity analysis.
7. Quality checks.
Reverse coding, missingness, internal consistency, item
total correlations, associations with stars and ride context, and a ve-item
single-factor CFA were examined. Fit is reported honestly; the CFA does not
establish structural validity.
Figure 5.9: Field survey ow. Panel A: ride context and overall star rating. Panel
B: broadcaster-rigour and perceived-benet ratings. Panel C: session completion
and separation of the anonymous research record from the LOKLOK prole. The
screens document the eld instrument, not additional outcome data.
5.6.2
Calibration Procedure
1. Document collection. The raw 60 questionnaires, 60 ride/PO narratives,
the 160-segment FGD transcript, the 185-segment self-report transcript, the</p>
<p>CHAPTER 5. METHODOLOGY
113
four corpora, and the 25 programme briefs were assembled.
2. Semantic features. Documents were embedded with multilingual BGE-M3
(1,024 dimensions) and reduced by PCA to 32 components. The ve semantic
blocks (PO, FGD, self-report, corpora, stimuli) were summarised into 167
features. Adding the 31 quantitative features gave the 198-feature matrix.
3. Debiasing.
DML residualised each feature on the nuisance block (person
mean and SD, acquiescence, ride duration, interaction, high-interaction indi-
cator, age, and gender). IPW reweighted the high-interaction stratum. The
diagnostics report balance before DML, after DML, and after IPW; IPW is
not assumed to improve monotonically.
4. Manifold. PCA, LLE, and Laplacian eigenmaps produced a six-axis spec-
tral manifold. The axes are a smoothing device for persona generation, not
uniquely identied psychological dimensions.
5. Persona prior. A Gaussian KDE was tted to the 52 debiased rows in the
six-axis space. Five hundred personas were drawn from this prior and mapped
to six traits: initial opinion, prior BRS, echo susceptibility, heat capacity,
critical scrutiny, and co-hosting preference.
6. Scale calibration. The status-quo arm G0 was anchored to the measured
PB mean (5.119), because the measured students used ordinary Uber, not the
LOKLOK epistemic features. This is a single scale anchor, not a t to the
treatment contrast.</p>
<p>CHAPTER 5. METHODOLOGY
114
5.6.3
In-Silico Procedure
1. Design. Every persona was cloned into the 23 factorial design: A = BRS
credibility architecture, B = MORE diversity/cooling, C = ERIC modera-
tion/reection. Each cell ran 18 listening sessions.
2. Mechanisms. The agent-based model implemented eight lters (Hamilto-
nian, Laplace, Lyapunov, DeGroot, Lagrange, Hilbert, Percolation, Entropy)
and the Zewail-inspired attentional gate. Ablations later showed that only
DeGroot, Lagrange, and Hilbert are outcome-active in this wiring; the thesis
reports them as eight implemented, not eight active, lters.
3. Outcomes. Terminal states were scored through the GRM-IRT measurement
model for evaluative ratings and the synthetic NMLS competence prole. The
simulator seed was 20261105; the run used 500 personas and 18 sessions.
4. Calibration check. G0 was anchored to measured PB; G1 was compared
on the same scale. The measured wave informed the persona prior, so the
comparison is calibration, not independent validation.
5. Inference and robustness.
Frequentist paired contrasts, a one-sample
Bayesian check, a 256-point joint mechanism/weight sweep, three topology
variants, three persona priors, a leave-one-lter-out ablation, a source
ablation, and a bootstrap over the 52 anchors were reported.
6. Reproducibility. The seed, code, cached embeddings, transcript, and gener-
ated outputs are included; the appendix gives the commands.
The details of the semantic, debiasing, manifold, and factorial steps are in
Section 5.5; the results are in Chapter 6.</p>
<p>CHAPTER 5. METHODOLOGY
115
5.6.4
Plain-Language Guide to the Formal Steps
The formal parts answer simple questions:
 DML. Predict each feature from the nuisance variables (demographics, re-
sponse style, ride context) and keep the residual. In plain terms: remove what
is explained by the person and the ride before looking at what is left.
 IPW. Reweight the sample so the high-interaction group is not overrepre-
sented. In plain terms: give the quiet and the talkative riders a comparable
say.
 PCA, LLE, and Laplacian eigenmaps. Compress 198 columns into six
axes that preserve global spread, local neighbourhoods, and network smooth-
ness. In plain terms: summarise the data without losing the shape.
 Q0 and personas. Fit a smooth distribution to the 52 debiased rows and
draw synthetic proles from it.
In plain terms: create plausible synthetic
listeners that sit inside the measured cloud, not 500 new humans.
 Factorial ABM. Test all eight on/o combinations in the same persona across
18 sessions.
In plain terms: compare the design bundles within the same
synthetic person, so dierences are not due to dierent people.
 GRM-IRT. Turn a latent score into item responses with thresholds. In plain
terms: translate a person's standing into the kind of rating they would give
on a ve- or seven-point item.
