6.12.13
Balanced Evidence Synthesis
The primary analysis uses the 52 de-duplicated records. A 60-row all-questionnaire
sensitivity gives ∆SReval = +0.808 [0.760, 0.856] versus +0.827 [0.779, 0.875] for the
52-row primary, and ∆SRcomp = +10.95 versus +11.30. PB reliability is essentially
unchanged (α = .947 in both), and the measured PB mean moves from 5.119 to
5.150. The exclusion rule therefore changes magnitudes only slightly; the 52-row
result is not an artefact of dropping the eight duplicate-vector records.
The held-out predictive comparison is more consequential. Table 6.24 shows
that psychometrics alone predict PB best (R2 = .915) and star ratings best (R2 =
.415). Adding demographic/context variables lowers both. Adding the 167 semantic
features lowers PB R2 to .583 and star R2 to .153, and the semantic-only model has
negative R2. In this sample, the BGE-M3/PCA block does not improve held-out
prediction of the measured outcomes. With 52 rows and 167 semantic features, that
result is expected from overtting alone; it is not evidence that the semantic layer
is uninformative in a larger sample. The quantitative block is the safer predictor
here, not a proven substitute for semantic measurement.
A content-supply stress test sets the poor-content share to 0.763, a heuristic
mapping of the Uber-review star mean of 1.69. This is an arbitrary stress param-
eter, not an estimated radio-propaganda rate: app reviews do not measure radio
misinformation. Running the main simulator with that content distribution lowers
∆SReval from +0.827 to +0.636, a paired dierence of −0.191 [−0.261, −0.122].
Content supply therefore changes the magnitude of the implied contrast, although
the sign remains positive.</p>
