6.12.15
Human Coding and Machine Validation
A separate validation test asked whether the 25-code qualitative codebook could
be applied reliably, and whether a local language model could stand in for human
coders. Three independent coders labelled the same 193 usable segments from the
FGD and self-report corpus. Pairwise Cohen's κ across the 25 binary codes was .762,
.852, and .861, and the majority label formed the human gold. Per-code agreement
is more variable than the pooled gure. Median pairwise κ ranged from .28 (C02) to
.95 (I07); C01 (.57), C02 (.28), C03 (.57), and I04 (.59) fell below .60, and C08 had
no positive labels and is not estimable. The ve mechanism codes were uneven: C04
(.91 to 1.00) and C05 (.66 to .88) were the most consistent, but C04 had only ve
positives and C01 to C03 had one or two. A leave-one-out check, in which each coder
was scored against the agreement of the other two, gave human micro-F1 of .991,
.989, and .998 and macro-F1 of .936, .925, and .987; because about 97 percent of
the 4,825 code cells are negative, these ceilings mainly reect agreement on absence.
Per-code values are in the supplementary CSV.
The archive contains three separately completed workbooks and the writ-
ten coding instructions. It does not record coder identities or a formal blinding
attestation, and it cannot establish that coders were blind to one another. The
majority-gold le was present in the bundle supplied to the machine-coding note-
book, so the comparison is not leakage-free; out-of-fold exemplar retrieval and the
exact-quote check limit, but do not eliminate, this risk.
Agreement is high in aggregate, but the per-code values show where the
codebook is weak. The validation supports the codebook as an applicable instrument</p>
<p>CHAPTER 6. RESULTS
211
for these segments. It does not validate the six rider-record themes, which remain
a single-researcher semantic analysis of a dierent corpus, and it does not make the
coded segments a probability sample.
The local arm used Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-AWQ with vLLM on a T4 GPU;
all text stayed on the machine. Both local passes coded the 193 human-gold units.
The zero-shot pass reached micro-F1 .193 over the 25-code matrix, and 32.0% of
its evidence quotes were not exact substrings of the source. A ve-fold out-of-fold
pass, in which one human exemplar was retrieved from the other folds for each
target, raised micro-F1 to .320 and lowered exact-quote fabrication to 16.1%. The
improvement is material, but the conditioned pass remains below the Claude rst
pass at .452. The folds contained 39, 39, 39, 38, and 38 units, and every human-
coded segment was predicted exactly once.
No mechanism code met the usability rule in either local pass. C01 to C04
had fewer than eight positive reference segments. C05, the only mechanism code
with eight positives, reached κ = .259 zero-shot and .323 with gold examples, and
was dropped in both.
C04 reached .431 zero-shot and .659 with gold examples
but had only ve reference positives, so it remained insucient to validate. The
strongest inductive code, I01 (radio-use context), reached .350 zero-shot and .431
with gold examples, which was descriptive only; I07 (social-media use) reached .000
and .268 and was dropped. No Qwen condition produced a usable code.
A rst-pass Claude coding was stronger at micro-F1 .452. I01 (κ = .667)
and I07 (κ = .611) were usable; C12, I03, I06, and I13 were descriptive only. Every
mechanism code C01 to C05 failed the same gate: C05 was dropped at κ = .390,</p>
<p>CHAPTER 6. RESULTS
212
and the others had too few reference positives. The mechanism layer was therefore
not eligible for E1 to E3.
The negative result is informative. Human coders can apply the codebook
consistently, but a 7B local model does not reproduce those judgments at code level
even when it receives human-gold exemplars. The failure is strongest where the
thesis most needs qualitative leverage: the ve assumption-linked mechanism codes.
Reporting the failure prevents a machine-labelled corpus from being presented as
convergent qualitative support. It also explains why the qualitative layer remains
an assumption audit and a source of candidate design vocabulary, not a calibration
