<sec id="ch12"><title>Measurement Invariance: Follow-Up Procedure</title>
<p>Appendix D
Measurement Invariance: Follow-Up Proce-
dure
Cross-group latent-mean comparisons require evidence that the measurement
model functions comparably across groups. This appendix species the follow-up
procedure for the human instrument. It does not report a human invariance analysis.
The v8 synthetic audit in Section 6.12.7 does not estimate multigroup invariance
for the current state-mediated item matrices; the earlier lavaan audit is not reused
because it belongs to a dierent synthetic matrix. The poor pooled t of the human
BRS must still be addressed before a human multigroup model can be assumed.
1. Congural model. Specify the same substantively justied factor structure
in each group and evaluate model t and identication.
2. Metric model.
Constrain corresponding factor loadings and assess the
change in t alongside parameter estimates and uncertainty.
3. Scalar model. For continuous indicators, constrain corresponding intercepts;
for ordinal items, use suitable threshold constraints and model identication
rules. Evaluate whether latent means are interpretable.
Putnick and Bornstein (2016) discuss the reporting and interpretation of
these steps. Fit-change criteria must be chosen for the estimator, model, sample,
259</p>
